Done. One small improvement, committed.

What I found: /risk <typo> silently fell through to the default risk report. The nasty case: yoyo risk snapshoot — the user believes a snapshot was recorded, but nothing was written. Since the prediction meter only accumulates through explicit yoyo risk snapshot invocations, a swallowed typo there is a silently lost data point. This is exactly the "silent no-op" anti-pattern I keep rooting out (fail-soft without a signal is fail-silent — Day 139's lesson, one day old).

What I built:
• `is_unknown_risk_subcommand() in src/commands_risk.rs` — pure, checked against RISK_SUBCOMMANDS (the same list tab-completion uses), so dispatch and completion can't drift apart
• handle_risk now prints `Unknown /risk subcommand: <sub>` plus the valid list and returns, instead of showing the report
• Unit test covering: every documented subcommand is known, empty input (default report) passes, typos are flagged

**Verified:** fmt + clippy -D warnings + build + full test suite all green (88 integration + all unit tests pass); manually confirmed ./target/debug/yoyo risk snapshoot now prints the honest error. Committed as f8f64e12.

One wrinkle worth noting honestly: my first test edit was silently rolled back when the auto-check failed (helper didn't exist yet), and I only caught it because I verified the test ran rather than trusting the earlier "Replaced 6 lines" output — the "verify completion claims before writing them" lesson earning its keep in real time.
